-- Q7 [Common Oxidizing & Reducing Agents · medium · Skill 3 · 4E Stoichiometry (GC) · ans A] (g33)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Oxidation-Reduction Reactions', 'Common Oxidizing & Reducing Agents',
    $qt33$A laboratory chemist is ranking the four common diatomic halogens by their strength as oxidizing agents. Recall that an oxidizing agent is the species that is itself reduced during a redox reaction, and that the greater a species' tendency to be reduced, the stronger an oxidizing agent it is. Among elemental \(\text{F}_2\), \(\text{Cl}_2\), \(\text{Br}_2\), and \(\text{I}_2\), which one is the strongest oxidizing agent, and on what basis?$qt33$,
    $op33$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{F}_2\\), because it has the strongest tendency among the halogens to be reduced, gaining electrons to form \\(\\text{F}^-\\) (each fluorine going from \\(0\\) to \\(-1\\))."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{I}_2\\), because iodine has the largest atomic radius among the halogens, so \\(\\text{I}_2\\) most readily accepts electrons to form \\(\\text{I}^-\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{F}_2\\), because it has the strongest tendency among the halogens to be oxidized, losing electrons to form \\(\\text{F}^+\\) (each fluorine going from \\(0\\) to \\(+1\\))."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{I}_2\\), because it is the most easily oxidized halogen, and the most easily oxidized species is by definition the strongest oxidizing agent."}]$op33$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex33$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the oxidizing agent in a redox reaction is the species that is reduced (it accepts electrons and causes the oxidation of something else), so the strongest oxidizing agent is the species with the greatest tendency to be reduced. Among the halogens, the half-reaction \(\text{X}_2 + 2e^- \rightarrow 2\,\text{X}^-\) has its most positive standard reduction potential for fluorine: \(\text{F}_2 + 2e^- \rightarrow 2\,\text{F}^-\) at \(E^\circ = +2.87\ \text{V}\), versus \(\text{Cl}_2\) at \(+1.36\ \text{V}\), \(\text{Br}_2\) at \(+1.07\ \text{V}\), and \(\text{I}_2\) at \(+0.54\ \text{V}\). In this reduction each fluorine atom gains one electron, dropping from an oxidation state of \(0\) in elemental \(\text{F}_2\) to \(-1\) in \(\text{F}^-\). Because \(\text{F}_2\) is reduced more readily than any other halogen, it is the strongest common oxidizing agent. (Choice B) Although \(\text{I}_2\) is correctly described as having the largest atomic radius, the relationship is backward: the large, weakly held valence shell of iodine gives \(\text{I}_2\) the lowest tendency to be reduced (\(E^\circ = +0.54\ \text{V}\)), making it the weakest oxidizing agent of the four, not the strongest. (Choice C) This choice identifies \(\text{F}_2\) but misstates the process: an oxidizing agent is reduced, not oxidized. Fluorine, the most electronegative element, gains an electron to reach \(-1\); it does not lose an electron to form \(\text{F}^+\) (a species that does not occur in ordinary chemistry), so the oxidation-state change described here is the reverse of what defines an oxidizing agent. (Choice D) This choice inverts the definition: the species most easily oxidized (most easily losing electrons) is the strongest reducing agent, not the strongest oxidizing agent. The strongest oxidizing agent is instead the species most easily reduced, so naming the most easily oxidized halogen contradicts the role being asked about. Skill 3$ex33$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc33$Stoichiometry (GC)$cc33$, 'general-chemistry', 3, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a33B$Cites iodine's largest atomic radius as making it most easily reduced, reversing the actual trend in which the largest halogen is the weakest oxidizing agent$a33B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a33C$Correctly picks F2 but claims the oxidizing agent is oxidized to F+, confusing being reduced with being oxidized$a33C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a33D$Defines the strongest oxidizing agent as the most easily oxidized species, swapping the definitions of oxidizing and reducing agent$a33D$ FROM q;

-- Q8 [Common Oxidizing & Reducing Agents · medium · Skill 2 · 4E Stoichiometry (GC) · ans B] (g34)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Oxidation-Reduction Reactions', 'Common Oxidizing & Reducing Agents',
    $qt34$A reagent consists of finely divided palladium metal supported on \(\text{CaCO}_3\) that has been treated with lead salts and quinoline, then exposed to \(\text{H}_2\) gas in the presence of an internal alkyne \(\text{R}-\text{C}\equiv\text{C}-\text{R}\). Exactly one equivalent of \(\text{H}_2\) is taken up. Which statement correctly identifies this reagent and the oxidation-number bookkeeping for the carbons and hydrogens involved when one \(\text{H}_2\) is added?$qt34$,
    $op34$[{"label":"A","text":"It is Lindlar's catalyst; each former alkyne carbon loses a bond to hydrogen and is oxidized (its oxidation number rises by \\(1\\)), while each H of \\(\\text{H}_2\\) goes from \\(0\\) to \\(-1\\), for a net transfer of \\(2\\) electrons per \\(\\text{H}_2\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"It is Lindlar's catalyst; each former alkyne carbon gains one new \\(\\text{C}-\\text{H}\\) bond and is reduced (its oxidation number drops by \\(1\\)), while each H of \\(\\text{H}_2\\) goes from \\(0\\) to \\(+1\\), for a net transfer of \\(2\\) electrons per \\(\\text{H}_2\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"It is Lindlar's catalyst; each former alkyne carbon gains one new \\(\\text{C}-\\text{H}\\) bond and is reduced (its oxidation number drops by \\(1\\)), while each H of \\(\\text{H}_2\\) goes from \\(0\\) to \\(-1\\), for a net transfer of \\(4\\) electrons per \\(\\text{H}_2\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"It is Lindlar's catalyst; each former alkyne carbon is unchanged in oxidation number because \\(\\text{C}-\\text{H}\\) bonds are nonpolar, while each H of \\(\\text{H}_2\\) goes from \\(0\\) to \\(+1\\), for a net transfer of \\(2\\) electrons per \\(\\text{H}_2\\)."}]$op34$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex34$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the reagent (Pd on \(\text{CaCO}_3\) deactivated with lead salts and quinoline) is Lindlar's catalyst, and the oxidation-number bookkeeping for adding one \(\text{H}_2\) across the triple bond is as follows. Oxidation numbers are assigned by giving each shared bonding pair to the more electronegative atom: in a \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bond, carbon (\(2.55\) on the Pauling scale) is more electronegative than hydrogen (\(2.20\)), so the bonding pair is assigned to carbon, meaning each \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bond contributes \(-1\) to carbon and \(+1\) to that hydrogen. When \(\text{H}_2\) adds, each of the two former alkyne carbons forms exactly one new bond to hydrogen, so each carbon's oxidation number decreases by \(1\) (the carbons are reduced). Each hydrogen atom in \(\text{H}_2\) starts at \(0\) (an element) and ends bonded to carbon at \(+1\), so each H is oxidized by losing one electron. With two H atoms changing from \(0\) to \(+1\), the net electron transfer is \(2\) electrons per \(\text{H}_2\) molecule, matched by the two carbons each gaining one electron. Thus \(\text{H}_2\) is the reducing agent and the alkyne is reduced. (Choice A) This reverses the direction of every change: carbons gaining \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bonds are reduced, not oxidized, and hydrogen bonded to the less-electronegative-than-carbon position is \(+1\), not \(-1\) (hydrogen is only \(-1\) in hydrides such as \(\text{NaH}\) where it is bonded to a less electronegative element), so this option inverts both the carbon and hydrogen oxidation-number trends. (Choice C) The carbon assignment is correct, but assigning each hydrogen an oxidation number of \(-1\) is wrong because carbon is more electronegative than hydrogen; this also inflates the electron count to \(4\) per \(\text{H}_2\) by double-counting a change of \(2\) per H that does not occur, mixing up the per-atom and per-molecule bookkeeping. (Choice D) Treating \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bonds as nonpolar leaves carbon's oxidation number unchanged, but oxidation-number rules assign the shared pair entirely to the more electronegative atom rather than splitting it, so the carbons do change (each by \(-1\)); the stated H change of \(0\) to \(+1\) is correct, which would be inconsistent with no change at carbon since electrons are conserved. Skill 2$ex34$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc34$Stoichiometry (GC)$cc34$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
