-- Q9 [Common Oxidizing & Reducing Agents · hard · Skill 4 · 4E Stoichiometry (GC) · ans C] (g35)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Oxidation-Reduction Reactions', 'Common Oxidizing & Reducing Agents',
    $qt35$Copper metal does not react with hydrochloric acid, yet it dissolves readily in concentrated nitric acid, \(\text{HNO}_3\), producing a brown gas. A student attributes this difference to the behavior of the acids' protons. Which statement best explains the dissolution of copper in concentrated \(\text{HNO}_3\) and correctly identifies the brown gas produced?$qt35$,
    $op35$[{"label":"A","text":"The dissolving agent is nitrogen in the nitrate ion, whose oxidation state falls from \\(+5\\) to \\(+2\\) as it is reduced to \\(\\text{NO}\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"The dissolving agent is the \\(\\text{H}^+\\) ion, which is reduced from \\(+1\\) to \\(0\\), liberating \\(\\text{H}_2\\) gas just as in a non-oxidizing acid."},{"label":"C","text":"The dissolving agent is the nitrate ion, whose nitrogen is reduced as it is converted to the brown gas \\(\\text{NO}_2\\), while the copper is oxidized to \\(\\text{Cu}^{2+}\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"The dissolving agent is nitrogen in the nitrate ion, which is oxidized from \\(+5\\) to \\(+6\\), and the brown gas is \\(\\text{NO}_2\\)."}]$op35$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex35$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the oxidizing power of nitric acid comes from the nitrogen center, not from \(\text{H}^+\). In \(\text{HNO}_3\) the nitrogen oxidation state is fixed by the rest of the formula: each O is \(-2\) (total \(-6\)) and H is \(+1\), so for the neutral molecule N must be \(+5\). Copper lies below hydrogen in the activity series, so \(\text{H}^+\) cannot oxidize it; this is why copper is inert toward \(\text{HCl}\). Concentrated \(\text{HNO}_3\) succeeds because the \(+5\) nitrogen of the nitrate ion is itself a strong oxidizer. With concentrated acid the reduction product is \(\text{NO}_2\): in \(\text{NO}_2\) the two O atoms are \(-2\) each (\(-4\) total) on a neutral molecule, so N is \(+4\). Nitrogen therefore drops from \(+5\) to \(+4\), gaining one electron per N. The balanced half-reactions are oxidation \(\text{Cu} \rightarrow \text{Cu}^{2+} + 2\text{e}^-\) and reduction \(\text{NO}_3^- + 2\text{H}^+ + \text{e}^- \rightarrow \text{NO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O}\); combining them (doubling the reduction) gives \(\text{Cu} + 4\text{HNO}_3 \rightarrow \text{Cu(NO}_3)_2 + 2\text{NO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O}\), and the \(\text{NO}_2\) accounts for the observed brown gas. (Choice A) names the correct oxidizing center and a real reduction product but gives the wrong species for concentrated acid: \(\text{NO}\) (N at \(+2\)) is the product of dilute nitric acid, whereas concentrated \(\text{HNO}_3\) yields \(\text{NO}_2\), and \(\text{NO}\) is colorless rather than brown. (Choice B) is incorrect because \(\text{H}^+\) reduction to \(\text{H}_2\) is the mechanism of a non-oxidizing acid such as \(\text{HCl}\); copper cannot reduce \(\text{H}^+\), which is precisely why \(\text{HCl}\) fails to dissolve it, so this choice misattributes the oxidizing role to the proton. (Choice D) reverses the direction of the electron transfer: a substance that dissolves copper must be reduced, not oxidized, and nitrogen cannot reach \(+6\) here; furthermore an oxidation state of \(+6\) is inconsistent with the \(+4\) nitrogen actually present in \(\text{NO}_2\). Skill 4$ex35$,
    'hard', '4E', $cc35$Stoichiometry (GC)$cc35$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
