-- Q10 [Common Oxidizing & Reducing Agents · hard · Skill 4 · 4E Stoichiometry (GC) · ans D] (g36)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Oxidation-Reduction Reactions', 'Common Oxidizing & Reducing Agents',
    $qt36$A chemist treats 4-oxopentanoic acid (levulinic acid), \(\text{CH}_3\text{C(=O)CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{COOH}\), with one molar equivalent of sodium borohydride, \(\text{NaBH}_4\), in methanol at \(0\,^\circ\text{C}\). Lithium aluminum hydride, \(\text{LiAlH}_4\), reduces both functional groups in this molecule under analogous conditions. Given that \(\text{NaBH}_4\) is the reducing agent (donating hydride and being oxidized), which product predominates, and why?$qt36$,
    $op36$[{"label":"A","text":"Neither group is reduced; \\(\\text{NaBH}_4\\) instead acts as an oxidizing agent, accepting electrons from the carbonyls and being itself reduced."},{"label":"B","text":"Both the ketone and the carboxylic acid are reduced to alcohols, because any reagent that supplies hydride reduces every carbonyl in the molecule indiscriminately."},{"label":"C","text":"The carboxylic acid is reduced to a primary alcohol while the ketone is left intact, because the more electrophilic acid carbonyl is preferentially attacked by hydride."},{"label":"D","text":"The ketone is reduced to a secondary alcohol while the carboxylic acid is left intact, because \\(\\text{NaBH}_4\\) is a milder hydride donor that reduces aldehydes and ketones but not carboxylic acids."}]$op36$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex36$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because sodium borohydride is the reducing agent throughout: each \(\text{B}-\text{H}\) bond delivers a hydride ion (\(\text{H}^-\), formal oxidation state \(-1\)) to a carbonyl carbon. In donating hydride, \(\text{NaBH}_4\) is itself oxidized (boron, originally bonded to four reducing \(\text{H}\) atoms, ends up in \(\text{B}(\text{OR})\)-type species), which is the defining behavior of a reducing agent. However, \(\text{NaBH}_4\) is a comparatively mild hydride donor: it is reactive enough to add hydride to the relatively electrophilic carbon of aldehydes and ketones, but not to the much less electrophilic carbon of a carboxylic acid (whose carbonyl is deactivated by resonance donation from the \(-\text{OH}\) oxygen and which, under these conditions, is first deprotonated to the even less electrophilic carboxylate). Therefore the ketone of levulinic acid is reduced to a secondary alcohol, \(\text{CH}_3\text{CH(OH)CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{COOH}\), while the carboxylic acid survives. The stronger hydride donor \(\text{LiAlH}_4\) is required to reduce the carboxylic acid (and esters/amides) all the way to a primary alcohol, which is why it reduces both groups. (Choice A) This inverts the redox roles: a species that donates hydride to carbonyl carbons is oxidized and is the reducing agent, not an oxidizing agent; \(\text{NaBH}_4\) does not accept electrons from the carbonyls. (Choice B) This overgeneralizes: not every hydride source reduces every carbonyl. Reducing strength is graded, and \(\text{NaBH}_4\) specifically cannot reduce carboxylic acids, so reduction is chemoselective rather than indiscriminate. (Choice C) This reverses the true reactivity order. The carboxylic acid carbonyl is the harder, not the easier, group to reduce because resonance from the hydroxyl oxygen lowers its electrophilicity (and the acid is deprotonated to carboxylate), whereas the ketone is the group \(\text{NaBH}_4\) actually reduces. Skill 4$ex36$,
    'hard', '4E', $cc36$Stoichiometry (GC)$cc36$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
