UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a40C$uses an incorrect final oxidation state of -1 for nitrogen, undercounting the electron change$a40C$ FROM q;

-- Q15 [Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method) · medium · Skill 2 · 4E Stoichiometry (GC) · ans A] (g41)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Oxidation-Reduction Reactions', 'Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method)',
    $qt41$In acidic aqueous solution, sulfur dioxide can be oxidized to sulfate ion, \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\). A student writes the unbalanced skeleton for this oxidation half-reaction as \(\text{SO}_2 \rightarrow \text{SO}_4^{2-}\) and balances it using the half-reaction method (adding \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), \(\text{H}^+\), and \(e^-\) as needed for an acidic medium). Which of the following is the correctly balanced oxidation half-reaction?$qt41$,
    $op41$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{SO}_2 + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\rightarrow \\text{SO}_4^{2-} + 4\\text{H}^+ + 2e^-\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{SO}_2 \\rightarrow \\text{SO}_4^{2-} + 2e^-\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{SO}_2 + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\rightarrow \\text{SO}_4^{2-} + 4\\text{H}^+ + 4e^-\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{SO}_2 + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\rightarrow \\text{SO}_4^{2-} + 4\\text{H}^+\\)"}]$op41$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex41$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because sulfur is oxidized from \(+4\) in \(\text{SO}_2\) (oxygen is \(-2\), so \(\text{S} + 2(-2) = 0\) gives \(\text{S} = +4\)) to \(+6\) in \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\) (\(\text{S} + 4(-2) = -2\) gives \(\text{S} = +6\)), a loss of \(2\) electrons per sulfur. Applying the half-reaction method in acid: balance oxygen by adding \(2\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\) to the left (left has \(2\) O, right has \(4\) O), giving \(\text{SO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{SO}_4^{2-}\); balance hydrogen by adding \(4\,\text{H}^+\) to the right, giving \(\text{SO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{SO}_4^{2-} + 4\text{H}^+\); then balance charge by adding \(2e^-\) to the right. The mass balance is correct (S: \(1=1\); O: \(2+2=4\); H: \(4=4\)) and the charge balance is correct (left \(=0\); right \(= -2 + 4 - 2 = 0\)). (Choice B) This skeleton conserves neither oxygen nor hydrogen: the left side has \(2\) oxygens while the right side has \(4\), and no \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) or \(\text{H}^+\) is included to account for the added oxygen, so mass is not balanced. (Choice C) The atoms are balanced, but the electron count is wrong: sulfur only changes by \(2\) units of oxidation state (\(+4\) to \(+6\)), so only \(2\) electrons are released; writing \(4e^-\) also leaves the charge unbalanced (left \(=0\), right \(= -2 + 4 - 4 = -2\)). (Choice D) The atoms are balanced, but no electrons are shown; without the \(2e^-\) on the right the charge is unbalanced (left \(=0\), right \(= -2 + 4 = +2\)) and the species lost during oxidation is not represented. Skill 2$ex41$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc41$Stoichiometry (GC)$cc41$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
