-- Q16 [Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method) · medium · Skill 2 · 4E Stoichiometry (GC) · ans B] (g42)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Oxidation-Reduction Reactions', 'Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method)',
    $qt42$When permanganate ion is reduced to manganese(IV) oxide in basic solution, the balanced half-reaction is written as \(\text{MnO}_4^- + x\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{MnO}_2 + 4\,\text{OH}^-\). What is the value of the coefficient \(x\) on \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) as a reactant?$qt42$,
    $op42$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(1\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(2\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(3\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(4\\)"}]$op42$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex42$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because manganese is reduced from \(+7\) in \(\text{MnO}_4^-\) (each O is \(-2\), so \(\text{Mn} + 4(-2) = -1 \Rightarrow \text{Mn} = +7\)) to \(+4\) in \(\text{MnO}_2\) (\(\text{Mn} + 2(-2) = 0 \Rightarrow \text{Mn} = +4\)), a gain of \(3\) electrons. Balancing first in acid gives \(\text{MnO}_4^- + 4\,\text{H}^+ + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{MnO}_2 + 2\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\). Converting to base, add \(4\,\text{OH}^-\) to both sides to neutralize the \(4\,\text{H}^+\): the \(4\,\text{H}^+ + 4\,\text{OH}^-\) become \(4\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\) on the left, leaving \(\text{MnO}_4^- + 4\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{MnO}_2 + 2\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + 4\,\text{OH}^-\). Canceling \(2\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\) common to both sides yields \(\text{MnO}_4^- + 2\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{MnO}_2 + 4\,\text{OH}^-\), so \(x = 2\). Checking: O is \(4+2=6\) on the left and \(2+4=6\) on the right; H is \(4\) on each side; charge is \(-1 + (-3) = -4\) on the left and \(4(-1) = -4\) on the right. (Choice A) A coefficient of \(1\) leaves oxygen and hydrogen unbalanced (only \(2\) O and \(2\) H supplied by water, insufficient to produce \(4\,\text{OH}^-\) while balancing the lost oxygens) and reflects under-counting the water needed. (Choice C) A coefficient of \(3\) confuses the water coefficient with the \(3\) electrons transferred in the reduction, which are unrelated quantities. (Choice D) A coefficient of \(4\) mistakenly carries over the \(4\,\text{H}^+\) (or the \(4\,\text{OH}^-\)) count to water rather than performing the acid-to-base conversion and canceling the duplicated water molecules. Skill 2$ex42$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc42$Stoichiometry (GC)$cc42$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'partial_truth', $a42A$Under-balances water, leaving O and H atoms uncounted$a42A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a42C$Sets water coefficient equal to the electron count (3 e-)$a42C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a42D$Carries the 4 H+/4 OH- count onto water without canceling duplicates$a42D$ FROM q;

-- Q17 [Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method) · medium · Skill 2 · 4E Stoichiometry (GC) · ans C] (g43)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Oxidation-Reduction Reactions', 'Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method)',
    $qt43$In strongly basic solution, chromium(III) hydroxide is oxidized to chromate ion according to the unbalanced half-reaction \(\text{Cr(OH)}_3 \rightarrow \text{CrO}_4^{2-}\). When this half-reaction is balanced in base using the half-reaction method (balancing O with \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) and H with \(\text{OH}^-\)), how many hydroxide ions \((\text{OH}^-)\) appear, and on which side do they appear?$qt43$,
    $op43$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(4\\;\\text{OH}^-\\) on the product (right) side"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(3\\;\\text{OH}^-\\) on the reactant (left) side"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(5\\;\\text{OH}^-\\) on the reactant (left) side"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(8\\;\\text{OH}^-\\) on the reactant (left) side"}]$op43$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex43$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because balancing the half-reaction in base yields \(5\;\text{OH}^-\) on the reactant side. Chromium is \(+3\) in \(\text{Cr(OH)}_3\) and \(+6\) in \(\text{CrO}_4^{2-}\), so \(3\) electrons are lost per chromium. Balancing stepwise: Cr is already \(1{:}1\). Oxygen goes from \(3\) (left) to \(4\) (right), so add one \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) to the left, giving \(\text{H}_2\text{O} + \text{Cr(OH)}_3 \rightarrow \text{CrO}_4^{2-}\), which now has \(4\) O on each side. Hydrogen is then \(5\) on the left (\(2\) from water plus \(3\) from hydroxide) and \(0\) on the right, so add \(5\;\text{H}^+\) to the right (acidic intermediate): \(\text{H}_2\text{O} + \text{Cr(OH)}_3 \rightarrow \text{CrO}_4^{2-} + 5\text{H}^+\). Charge is \(0\) on the left and \(+3\) on the right, so add \(3\;e^-\) to the right: \(\text{H}_2\text{O} + \text{Cr(OH)}_3 \rightarrow \text{CrO}_4^{2-} + 5\text{H}^+ + 3e^-\) (the \(3\) electrons confirm the \(+3 \rightarrow +6\) oxidation). To convert to base, add \(5\;\text{OH}^-\) to both sides to neutralize the \(5\;\text{H}^+\): \(5\text{OH}^- + \text{H}_2\text{O} + \text{Cr(OH)}_3 \rightarrow \text{CrO}_4^{2-} + 5\text{H}_2\text{O} + 3e^-\). Cancelling the one \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) on the left against one of the five on the right gives the final balanced half-reaction \(5\text{OH}^- + \text{Cr(OH)}_3 \rightarrow \text{CrO}_4^{2-} + 4\text{H}_2\text{O} + 3e^-\), with \(5\;\text{OH}^-\) on the reactant side. (Choice A) \(4\;\text{OH}^-\) on the right confuses the hydroxide coefficient with the coefficient of \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) (\(4\;\text{H}_2\text{O}\)), which appears on the product side; the hydroxide ions are added to neutralize \(\text{H}^+\) and end up on the reactant side. (Choice B) \(3\;\text{OH}^-\) on the left mistakes the hydroxide coefficient for the number of electrons transferred (\(3\;e^-\) from the \(+3 \rightarrow +6\) change); the electron count and the \(\text{OH}^-\) count are determined by separate balancing steps and need not match. (Choice D) \(8\;\text{OH}^-\) results from failing to cancel the common \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) and instead summing all hydrogen-bearing species, overcounting the hydroxide added during neutralization. Skill 2$ex43$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc43$Stoichiometry (GC)$cc43$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
