UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a43B$Mistakes OH- coefficient for the electron-transfer count (3 e-)$a43B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a43D$Fails to cancel common water, overcounting added hydroxide$a43D$ FROM q;

-- Q18 [Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method) · hard · Skill 4 · 4E Stoichiometry (GC) · ans D] (g44)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Oxidation-Reduction Reactions', 'Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method)',
    $qt44$In an acidic titration, permanganate oxidizes oxalate \(\text{C}_2\text{O}_4^{2-}\) to carbon dioxide while \(\text{MnO}_4^-\) is reduced to \(\text{Mn}^{2+}\). A student balances the process with the half-reaction method, writing the reduction half-reaction as \(\text{MnO}_4^- + 8\text{H}^+ + 5e^- \rightarrow \text{Mn}^{2+} + 4\text{H}_2\text{O}\) and the oxidation half-reaction as \(\text{C}_2\text{O}_4^{2-} \rightarrow 2\text{CO}_2 + 2e^-\). After equalizing the electrons and combining the two half-reactions into a single overall equation written with the smallest whole-number coefficients, what is the coefficient of \(\text{H}^+\) in that overall equation?$qt44$,
    $op44$[{"label":"A","text":"5"},{"label":"B","text":"8"},{"label":"C","text":"10"},{"label":"D","text":"16"}]$op44$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex44$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the two half-reactions can be added only after the electrons lost equal the electrons gained, and the half-reaction containing \(\text{H}^+\) must itself be scaled to reach that common electron count. In the reduction half-reaction, manganese goes from \(+7\) in \(\text{MnO}_4^-\) to \(+2\) in \(\text{Mn}^{2+}\), a gain of 5 electrons: \(\text{MnO}_4^- + 8\text{H}^+ + 5e^- \rightarrow \text{Mn}^{2+} + 4\text{H}_2\text{O}\). In the oxidation half-reaction, each carbon in \(\text{C}_2\text{O}_4^{2-}\) goes from \(+3\) to \(+4\) in \(\text{CO}_2\), so the two carbons lose 2 electrons total: \(\text{C}_2\text{O}_4^{2-} \rightarrow 2\text{CO}_2 + 2e^-\). The least common multiple of 5 and 2 is 10, so the reduction half-reaction is multiplied by 2 and the oxidation half-reaction by 5: \(2\text{MnO}_4^- + 16\text{H}^+ + 10e^- \rightarrow 2\text{Mn}^{2+} + 8\text{H}_2\text{O}\) and \(5\text{C}_2\text{O}_4^{2-} \rightarrow 10\text{CO}_2 + 10e^-\). Adding these and cancelling the 10 electrons gives the overall equation \(2\text{MnO}_4^- + 16\text{H}^+ + 5\text{C}_2\text{O}_4^{2-} \rightarrow 2\text{Mn}^{2+} + 8\text{H}_2\text{O} + 10\text{CO}_2\). Because the original \(8\text{H}^+\) was multiplied by 2 when the reduction half-reaction was scaled, the \(\text{H}^+\) coefficient is 16, and the equation balances for all atoms and for charge \((+4\) on each side\()\) with no common factor to reduce. (Choice A) 5 is the coefficient of the oxalate ion \(\text{C}_2\text{O}_4^{2-}\) in the overall equation, not the coefficient of \(\text{H}^+\). (Choice B) 8 is the number of \(\text{H}^+\) in the unscaled reduction half-reaction; it ignores that this half-reaction must be doubled to supply 10 electrons, so its \(\text{H}^+\) must be doubled as well. (Choice C) 10 is the equalized number of electrons transferred (the least common multiple of 5 and 2), not the number of protons that appear in the balanced equation. Skill 4$ex44$,
    'hard', '4E', $cc44$Stoichiometry (GC)$cc44$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
