-- Q21 [Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method) · hard · Skill 3 · 4E Stoichiometry (GC) · ans C] (g47)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Oxidation-Reduction Reactions', 'Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method)',
    $qt47$In basic solution, elemental bromine disproportionates to bromide ion and bromate ion according to the unbalanced skeleton reaction \(\text{Br}_2 \rightarrow \text{Br}^- + \text{BrO}_3^-\). A student balances this process with the half-reaction method, balancing oxygen with \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) and hydrogen with \(\text{OH}^-\) as appropriate for basic medium, then equalizes the electrons and combines the half-reactions into a single overall equation written with the smallest whole-number coefficients. In that balanced overall equation, what is the coefficient of \(\text{OH}^-\)?$qt47$,
    $op47$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(3\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(12\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(6\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(5\\)"}]$op47$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex47$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because separating the disproportionation into half-reactions and balancing in base yields a coefficient of \(6\) on \(\text{OH}^-\). Bromine begins at oxidation state \(0\) in \(\text{Br}_2\). In the reduction half-reaction it falls to \(-1\): \(\text{Br}_2 + 2e^- \rightarrow 2\,\text{Br}^-\). In the oxidation half-reaction it rises to \(+5\); balancing O with water and H with hydroxide in base gives \(\text{Br}_2 + 12\,\text{OH}^- \rightarrow 2\,\text{BrO}_3^- + 6\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + 10e^-\). Multiplying the reduction half-reaction by \(5\) makes both half-reactions exchange \(10e^-\); adding them and cancelling electrons gives \(6\,\text{Br}_2 + 12\,\text{OH}^- \rightarrow 10\,\text{Br}^- + 2\,\text{BrO}_3^- + 6\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\). Dividing every coefficient by \(2\) to reach the smallest whole numbers gives \(3\,\text{Br}_2 + 6\,\text{OH}^- \rightarrow 5\,\text{Br}^- + \text{BrO}_3^- + 3\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\), in which \(\text{OH}^-\) carries a coefficient of \(6\). This balances: Br \(6 = 5 + 1\); O \(6 = 3 + 3\); H \(6 = 6\); and charge \(-6 = -6\) on each side. (Choice A) \(3\) is the coefficient of \(\text{Br}_2\) (and of \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\)) in the final balanced equation, not the coefficient of \(\text{OH}^-\). (Choice B) \(12\) is the \(\text{OH}^-\) coefficient in the combined equation before it is reduced to smallest whole numbers; failing to divide the entire equation by the common factor of \(2\) leaves every coefficient doubled. (Choice D) \(5\) is the coefficient of \(\text{Br}^-\) in the final balanced equation, not the coefficient of \(\text{OH}^-\). Skill 3$ex47$,
    'hard', '4E', $cc47$Stoichiometry (GC)$cc47$, 'general-chemistry', 3, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
